§5 — MODEL PARITY. 2026-08-04T05:25:36Z

THE TRAP, MEASURED AGAINST THE LIVE DATABASE. The corpus stores TWO strings for one model:
    section_embeddings.model_slug   'bge-large-en-v1.5'         362,948 rows
    embeddings.metadata->>'model'   'BAAI/bge-large-en-v1.5'  1,070,674 rows
and the tradition-gap join reads `embeddings`, because that is where `served` lives --
while migration 100 documented the user table as carrying the SHORT form. So 'compare the
slugs' has no single right answer and both natural implementations are wrong:
  * userRow.model_slug === EMBED_MODEL is TAUTOLOGICALLY GREEN -- writer and check read the
    same constant, so every user row passes while silently mismatching the corpus.
  * comparing to the short literal passes against section_embeddings and FAILS against every
    row of embeddings, which is the plane the join actually uses.
Resolved by making parity about the MODEL rather than the spelling, and by requiring the
caller to supply the corpus's OWN recorded value -- isJoinable has no default, so the
tautology is unrepresentable rather than merely discouraged.

NO API CALL IN THIS SUITE. Neither CI job supplies DEEPINFRA_API_KEY, so a parity check that
needed the network would never run where it matters. Every assertion is on stored values.

